{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhenhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangqi","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Xiangqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanpei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuanpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaodong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianglong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xianglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huijie","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Huijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weifeng","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Weifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Simin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RobustVLA: On Robustness of Vision-Language-Action Model against Multi-Modal Perturbations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T14:42:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T08:40:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:36:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T09:55:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:52:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T07:21:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:28:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-19T04:43:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:48:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-27T06:20:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:15:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In Vision-Language-Actionf(VLA) models, robustness to real-world perturbations is critical for deployment. Existing methods target simple visual disturbances, overlooking the broader multi-modal perturbations that arise in actions, instructions, environments, and observations. Here, we first evaluate the robustness of mainstream VLAs under 17 perturbations across four modalities. We find (1) actions as the most fragile modality, (2) Existing visual-robust VLA do not gain robustness in other modality, and (3) pi0 demonstrates superior robustness. To build multi-modal robust VLAs, we propose RobustVLA against perturbations in VLA inputs and outputs. For output robustness, we perform offline robust optimization against worst-case action noise that maximizes mismatch in flow matching objective. This can be seen as adversarial training, label smoothing, and outlier penalization. For input robustness, we enforce consistent actions across input variations that preserve task semantics. To account for multiple perturbations, we formulate robustness as a multi-armed bandit problem and apply an upper confidence bound algorithm to automatically identify the most harmful noise. Experiments on LIBERO demonstrate our RobustVLA delivers absolute gains over baselines of 12.6% on the pi0 backbone and 10.4% on the OpenVLA backbone across all 17 perturbations, achieving 50.6x faster inference than existing visual-robust BYOVLA that requires external LLMs, and a 10.4% gain under mixed perturbations. On the real-world FR5 robot, under four types of multimodal perturbations, RobustVLA shows strong low-data performance, outperforming pi0 by 65.6% success rate with 25 demonstrations. Even with abundant demos, our method still outperform pi0 by 30% success rate. Code and demo videos available at https://github.com/gakakulicc/RobustVLA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:53Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:48:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00054","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00054","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00054","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianjie","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xianjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiman","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yiman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixiong","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Yixiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liang","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Liang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HiDe: Rethinking The Zoom-IN method in High Resolution MLLMs via Hierarchical Decoupling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T08:31:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T17:51:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T01:20:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-04T08:28:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:42:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have made significant strides in visual understanding tasks. However, their performance on high-resolution images remains suboptimal. While existing approaches often attribute this limitation to perceptual constraints and argue that MLLMs struggle to recognize small objects, leading them to use \"zoom in\" strategies for better detail, our analysis reveals a different cause: the main issue is not object size, but rather caused by complex background interference. We systematically analyze this \"zoom in\" operation through a series of decoupling experiments and propose the Hierarchical Decoupling Framework (HiDe), a training-free framework that uses Token-wise Attention Decoupling (TAD) to decouple the question tokens and identify the key information tokens, then leverages their attention weights to achieve precise alignment with the target visual regions. Subsequently, it employs Layout-Preserving Decoupling (LPD) to decouple these regions from the background and reconstructs a compact representation that preserves essential spatial layouts while eliminating background interference. HiDe sets a new SOTA on V*Bench, HRBench4K, and HRBench8K, boosting Qwen2.5-VL 7B and InternVL3 8B to SOTA (92.1% and 91.6% on V*Bench), even surpassing RL methods. After optimization, HiDe uses 75% less memory than the previous training-free approach. Code is provided in https://tennine2077.github.io/HiDe.github.io/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICML2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00054","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:19Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-05T07:04:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun-ichiro","familyName":"Hayashi","name":"Hayashi, Shun-ichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koki","familyName":"Morita","name":"Morita, Koki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daichi","familyName":"Mukunoki","name":"Mukunoki, Daichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya","familyName":"Hoshino","name":"Hoshino, Tetsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takahiro","familyName":"Katagiri","name":"Katagiri, Takahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T04:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-05T02:24:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:17:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-12T14:08:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:52:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this study, we propose VibeCodeHPC, a multi-agent system based on large language models (LLMs) for the automatic tuning of high-performance computing (HPC) programs on supercomputers. VibeCodeHPC adopts Claude Code as its backend and provides an integrated environment that facilitates program development in supercomputer settings. The system not only brings the Vibe Coding paradigm -- program development through natural language interaction with users -- to HPC programming, but also enables autonomous performance optimization with minimal user intervention through a sophisticated multi-agent design. To achieve these objectives, VibeCodeHPC implements three core functionalities: (1) configuration capabilities tailored to the unique development environments of supercomputers, (2) collaborative operation among multiple LLM agents with distinct roles -- Project Manager (PM), System Engineer (SE), Programmer (PG), and Continuous Deliverer (CD), and (3) long-term autonomous operation through agent activity monitoring and dynamic deployment mechanisms. This paper highlights one of the most powerful features of VibeCodeHPC: fully automated code optimization through autonomous operation without user intervention. Specifically, it demonstrates the performance optimization of CPU-based codes on GPU-equipped systems for matrix multiplication and a Poisson equation solver using Jacobi's iterative method. The results show that the multi-agent configuration employed in VibeCodeHPC enables faster and more reliable development of higher-performance code compared to a single-agent setup."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:44Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00065","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00065","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00065","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdelrhman","familyName":"Gaber","name":"Gaber, Abdelrhman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad","familyName":"ElMahdy","name":"ElMahdy, Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youssif","familyName":"Abuzied","name":"Abuzied, Youssif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Abd-Eltawab","name":"Abd-Eltawab, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tamer","familyName":"ElBatt","name":"ElBatt, Tamer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FedLLM-Align: Feature Extraction From Heterogeneous Clients"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T14:06:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T03:36:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T19:35:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:03:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated learning (FL) enables collaborative model training without sharing raw data, making it attractive for privacy-sensitive domains, e.g., healthcare, finance, and IoT. A major obstacle, however, is the potential heterogeneity of tabular data across clients, in practical settings, where schema mismatches and incompatible feature spaces prevent straightforward aggregation. To address this challenge, this paper proposes FedLLM-Align, a federated learning framework that leverages pretrained transformer based language models for feature extraction. Towards this objective, FedLLM-Align serializes tabular records into text and derives semantically aligned embeddings from a pretrained LLM encoder, e.g, DistilBERT, facilitating lightweight local classifier heads that can be trained in a federated manner using standard aggregation schemes, e.g., FedAvg, while keeping all raw data records local. To quantify the merits and trade-offs of FedLLM-Align, we evaluate the proposed framework on binary classification tasks from two different domains: i) Coronary heart disease prediction on partitioned Framingham Heart Study data, and ii) Customer churn prediction on a financial dataset. FedLLM-Align outperforms state-of-the-art baselines by up to 25% in terms of the F1 score, under simulated schema heterogeneity, and achieves a 65% reduction in the communication overhead. These results establish FedLLM-Align as a privacy-preserving and communication-efficient approach for federated training based on clients with heterogeneous tabular datasets, commonly encountered in practice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00065","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:35Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianghong","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jianghong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaofeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Uncovering Intrinsic Capabilities: A Paradigm for Data Curation in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T02:57:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-14T12:33:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T01:38:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large vision-language models (VLMs) achieve strong benchmark performance, but controlling their behavior through instruction tuning remains difficult. Reducing the budget of instruction tuning dataset often causes regressions, as heuristic strategies treat models as black boxes and overlook the latent capabilities that govern learning. We introduce Capability-Attributed Data Curation (CADC), a framework that shifts curation from task-specific heuristics to intrinsic capability analysis. CADC discovers intrinsic capabilities in an unsupervised manner from gradient-based learning trajectories, attributes training data to these capabilities via influence estimation, and curates capability-aware curricula through balanced selection and staged sequencing. This transforms black-box instruction tuning into a controllable, capability-driven process. With as little as 5% of the original data, CADC surpasses full-data training on multimodal benchmarks. These results validate intrinsic capabilities as the fundamental building blocks of model learning and establish CADC as a principle paradigm for instruction data curation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:58Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-15T03:10:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongqi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Dongqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARS: Adaptive Reasoning Suppression for Efficient Large Reasoning Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T20:19:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T15:04:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:55:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Reasoning Language Models (LRLMs or LRMs) demonstrate remarkable capabilities in complex reasoning tasks, but suffer from significant computational inefficiencies due to overthinking phenomena. Existing efficient reasoning methods face the challenge of balancing reasoning quality with inference cost reduction. We propose \\textbf{Adaptive Reasoning Suppression (ARS)}, a novel training-free approach that dynamically suppresses redundant reasoning steps while preserving accuracy through adaptive certainty monitoring. ARS introduces a multi-checkpoint certainty estimation mechanism with progressive suppression thresholds, achieving superior efficiency compared to static suppression methods. Our extensive evaluation across mathematical reasoning benchmarks using multiple model architectures demonstrates that ARS achieves up to 53%, 46.1%, and 57.9% in token, latency and energy reduction, while maintaining or improving accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by 39th NeurIPS - Foundations of Reasoning in Language Models"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:44Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:16:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MACE: A Hybrid LLM Serving System with Colocated SLO-aware Continuous Retraining Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-28T18:45:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:01:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) deployed on edge servers are increasingly used in latency-sensitive applications such as personalized assistants, recommendation, and content moderation. However, the non-stationary nature of user data necessitates frequent retraining, which introduces a fundamental tension between inference latency and model accuracy under constrained GPU resources. Existing retraining strategies either delay model updates, over-commit resources to retraining, or overlook iteration-level retraining granularity. In this paper, we identify that iteration-level scheduling is crucial for adapting retraining frequency to model drift without violating service-level objectives (SLOs). We propose MACE, a hybrid LLM system that colocates concurrent inference (prefill, decode) and fine-tuning, with intelligent memory management to maximize task performance while promising inference throughput. MACE leverages the insight that not all model updates equally affect output alignment and allocates GPU cycles accordingly to balance throughput, latency, and update freshness. Our trace-driven evaluation shows that MACE matches or exceeds continuous retraining while reducing inference latency by up to 63% and maintaining throughput under resource constraints. Compared to periodic retraining, MACE improves latency breakdown across prefill, decode, and finetune stages, and sustains GPU utilization above 85% in NVIDIA AGX Orin. These results demonstrate that iteration-level hybrid scheduling is a promising direction for deploying LLMs with continual learning capabilities on edge platforms."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 15 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:46:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:46:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:46:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02373","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.02373","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.02373","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianshan","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Qianshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tengchao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tengchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaochen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yaochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinfeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xinfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenfei","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Zhenfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thorsten","familyName":"Holz","name":"Holz, Thorsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqiang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhiqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"XiaoFeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, XiaoFeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A-MemGuard: A Proactive Defense Framework for LLM-Based Agent Memory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T16:04:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-06T00:01:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM) agents use memory to learn from past interactions, enabling autonomous planning and decision-making in complex environments. However, this reliance on memory introduces a critical security risk: an adversary can inject seemingly harmless records into an agent's memory to manipulate its future behavior. This vulnerability is characterized by two core aspects: First, the malicious effect of injected records is only activated within a specific context, making them hard to detect when individual memory entries are audited in isolation. Second, once triggered, the manipulation can initiate a self-reinforcing error cycle: the corrupted outcome is stored as precedent, which not only amplifies the initial error but also progressively lowers the threshold for similar attacks in the future. To address these challenges, we introduce A-MemGuard (Agent-Memory Guard), the first proactive defense framework for LLM agent memory. The core idea of our work is the insight that memory itself must become both self-checking and self-correcting. Without modifying the agent's core architecture, A-MemGuard combines two mechanisms: (1) consensus-based validation, which detects anomalies by comparing reasoning paths derived from multiple related memories and (2) a dual-memory structure, where detected failures are distilled into ``lessons'' stored separately and consulted before future actions, breaking error cycles and enabling adaptation. Comprehensive evaluations on multiple benchmarks show that A-MemGuard effectively cuts attack success rates by over 95% while incurring a minimal utility cost. This work shifts LLM memory security from static filtering to a proactive, experience-driven model where defenses strengthen over time. Our code is available in https://github.com/TangciuYueng/AMemGuard"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.02373","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-06T01:26:10Z","registered":"2025-10-06T01:26:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-06T01:26:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01256","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01256","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01256","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingling","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Lingling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Gen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kant: An Efficient Unified Scheduling System for Large-Scale AI Clusters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7; C.2.4; C.1.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T02:25:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:01:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As AI cluster sizes continue to expand and the demand for large-language-model (LLM) training and inference workloads grows rapidly, traditional scheduling systems face significant challenges in balancing resource utilization, scheduling efficiency, and service quality. This paper presents and evaluates Kant: an efficient unified scheduling platform designed for large-scale AI container clusters, supporting the co-scheduling of both training and inference jobs. Based on the practical implementation of the Kant system, we systematically define a set of key evaluation metrics for AI clusters, including GPU Allocation Ratio (GAR), Scheduling Occupancy Rate (SOR), GPU Node Fragmentation Ratio (GFR), Job Waiting Time Distribution (JWTD), and Job Training Time Estimation Distribution (JTTED), providing a foundation for quantitative performance analysis. Experimental results demonstrate that Kant achieves exceptional performance in clusters ranging from hundreds to tens of thousands of GPUs. By leveraging scheduling strategies such as Backfill and Enhanced Binpack (E-Binpack), the system significantly improves resource utilization and scheduling efficiency, while effectively reducing resource fragmentation and communication overhead in distributed training. The system has been deployed in multiple AI data center clusters, where it stably supports large-scale intelligent computing workloads. This work provides a practical engineering approach for building high-performance, highly available, AI-native scheduling infrastructure."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages,15 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01256","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-03T01:44:38Z","registered":"2025-10-03T01:44:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-03T01:44:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00047","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00047","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00047","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Sihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santosh","familyName":"Vasa","name":"Vasa, Santosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditi","familyName":"Ramadwar","name":"Ramadwar, Aditi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Explanation-Driven Counterfactual Testing for Faithfulness in Vision-Language Model Explanations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T15:16:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) often produce fluent Natural Language Explanations (NLEs) that sound convincing but may not reflect the causal factors driving predictions. This mismatch of plausibility and faithfulness poses technical and governance risks. We introduce Explanation-Driven Counterfactual Testing (EDCT), a fully automated verification procedure for a target VLM that treats the model's own explanation as a falsifiable hypothesis. Given an image-question pair, EDCT: (1) obtains the model's answer and NLE, (2) parses the NLE into testable visual concepts, (3) generates targeted counterfactual edits via generative inpainting, and (4) computes a Counterfactual Consistency Score (CCS) using LLM-assisted analysis of changes in both answers and explanations. Across 120 curated OK-VQA examples and multiple VLMs, EDCT uncovers substantial faithfulness gaps and provides regulator-aligned audit artifacts indicating when cited concepts fail causal tests."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 workshop on Regulatable ML"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00047","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:08Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:50:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaotian","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Xiaotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforcement Learning-Based Prompt Template Stealing for Text-to-Image Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T12:29:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) have transformed text-to-image workflows, allowing designers to create novel visual concepts with unprecedented speed. This progress has given rise to a thriving prompt trading market, where curated prompts that induce trademark styles are bought and sold. Although commercially attractive, prompt trading also introduces a largely unexamined security risk: the prompts themselves can be stolen.\n In this paper, we expose this vulnerability and present RLStealer, a reinforcement learning based prompt inversion framework that recovers its template from only a small set of example images. RLStealer treats template stealing as a sequential decision making problem and employs multiple similarity based feedback signals as reward functions to effectively explore the prompt space. Comprehensive experiments on publicly available benchmarks demonstrate that RLStealer gets state-of-the-art performance while reducing the total attack cost to under 13% of that required by existing baselines. Our further analysis confirms that RLStealer can effectively generalize across different image styles to efficiently steal unseen prompt templates. Our study highlights an urgent security threat inherent in prompt trading and lays the groundwork for developing protective standards in the emerging MLLMs marketplace."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:06Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:50:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Sitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking RoPE Scaling in Quantized LLM: Theory, Outlier, and Channel-Band Analysis with Weight Rescaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T01:23:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Extending the context window support of large language models (LLMs) is crucial for tasks with long-distance dependencies. RoPE-based interpolation and extrapolation methods, such as linear scaling and frequency-aware schemes, enable longer input length support without retraining, while post-training quantization (PTQ) makes deployment practical. However, we show that combining RoPE position interpolation (PI) with PTQ degrades accuracy due to coupled effects including long-context aliasing, dynamic-range dilation, anisotropy from axis-aligned quantizers vs. rotated RoPE pairs, and outlier shifting that produces position-dependent logit noise. We provide, to the best of our knowledge, the first systematic analysis of the PI+PTQ approach and introduce two practical diagnostics: interpolation pressure (per-band sensitivity to phase scaling) and tail-inflation ratios (outlier shift from short to long contexts). Following the analysis results, we propose Q-ROAR (Quantization, RoPE-interpolation, and Outlier Aware Rescaling), a weight-only, interpolation-aware stabilization of PI for quantized LLMs. Q-ROAR groups RoPE dimensions into a small number of frequency bands and performs a lightweight search over per-band scales for Key and Query weights (with an optional symmetric variant to preserve logit scale). The search is guided by our diagnostics and uses a tiny long-context development dataset, requiring no fine-tuning to the model, no architecture or kernel changes, and no additional deployment overhead. Empirically, Q-ROAR reduces the model's perplexity on long-context workloads by more than 14%, while preserving short-context performance, inference throughput, and compatibility with existing LLM system stacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:40Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:49:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"included":[{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients","attributes":{"name":"arXiv","symbol":"ARXIV.CONTENT","year":2021,"alternateName":null,"description":null,"language":[],"clientType":"repository","domains":"*","re3data":null,"opendoar":null,"issn":{},"url":null,"created":"2021-03-29T15:47:29Z","updated":"2024-11-05T11:34:27Z","isActive":true},"relationships":{"provider":{"data":{"id":"arxiv","type":"providers"}},"prefixes":{"data":[]}}}],"meta":{"total":12,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=%28doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.00028+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.00031+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.00037+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.00040+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.00046+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.00047+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.00054+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.00065+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.00071+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.02373+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.03283+OR+doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2510.01256%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026include=client"}}